  "response": "Ran `crimes context src/billing.ts` — 8 findings on the file (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), churn low, test gap bottom-quartile, 0 direct importers. Here's what I'd change, in order.\n\n## 1. Fix the refund block (lines 33–44) — three findings in twelve lines\n\n`crime_00013` (False Identity, conf 0.80), `crime_00019` (Logic in the Alibi, conf 0.71), and `crime_00021` (Commented-Out Corpse, conf 0.90) all land on the same twelve-line region. Read together they're worse than any of them alone:\n\n- Line 39 states a rule — *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves\"* — and the body at 40–44 enforces nothing. It takes `user`, never reads `user.plan`, never checks a role, and returns `invoice.totalCents` unconditionally. The detector's evidence says exactly this: *\"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing.\"* That's an unenforced authorization rule sitting in a money path.\n- `calculateRefundPreview` is named like a pure calculation but awaits `saveRefundAudit` and `sendRefundEmail` first. Anything that calls it \"just to preview\" writes an audit row and mails the customer.\n- The dead block at 33–37 sketches an *approve-and-receipt* flow. An agent looking for the refund implementation finds plausible-looking `loadRefund`/`approveRefund` code in a comment directly above a function that doesn't do it.\n\nFix: delete 33–37 (git has it), split into `refundPreviewCents(invoice)` (pure) and `recordRefundRequest(...)` (effects), and turn line 39's prose into a real guard — `assertCanRefund(user, invoice)` throwing on non-owner enterprise refunds. Small change, and it's the only one here that closes a correctness gap rather than a comprehension one.\n\n## 2. Split `generateInvoice` — `crime_00014`, the file's only high (sev 0.90, conf 0.95)\n\n204 lines, 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold, and the section banners the author wrote are themselves the seam list: identity, plan pricing, loyalty discount, promo, fraud scoring, tax, currency, persistence, audit log, metrics, email body, retry. Two things fall out once you cut along them:\n\n- The tax ladder at 145–154 is a verbatim copy of the exported `tax()` at 257–268. Nothing links them; a rate change hits one copy. Extracting `taxCents = tax(user.country, afterDiscount)` deletes the duplicate outright.\n- `crime_00040` (Catch and Release) and the PII `console.log` at 183–199 are only invisible because they're buried at line 245 and 184 of a 204-line body. The catch at 245–247 has no binding at all, so three \"retries\" of a mail call report success to the caller either way. Extracted into a `sendInvoiceEmail` that returns a result, the failure becomes something `metadata` can record honestly.\n\nOrder the extraction pure-first (`priceLines`, `loyaltyDiscount`, `applyPromo`, `scoreRisk`, `currencyFor`) and leave `generateInvoice` as a thin orchestrator. Each helper is then testable without a mail service.\n\n## 3. Inject a clock — `crime_00012` (7 uses, conf 0.90), the file's highest agent risk (0.34)\n\n`Date.now()`/`new Date()` at 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273. This is the reason the file can't be tested, and the tests confirm it: `crime_00025` says `src/billing.test.ts` has a test with **zero** assertions, `crime_00031` says the other only uses a weak matcher. You can't assert on `generateInvoice`'s output while `metadata.runAt`, `createdAt`, and `emailLatencyMs` change every run — and the loyalty discount at 92–102 is *derived from wall time*, so the same user crosses the 90/180/365-day boundaries mid-test-suite.\n\nThread a `now: () => number` through `generateInvoice` and `recommendUpgrade` (273 has the same `daysSince` computation — that duplication is worth collapsing into one `daysSince(user, now)` while you're there). Line 56's `Math.random()` in the invoice id has the identical problem and isn't flagged by this detector; inject an id factory alongside the clock.\n\n---\n\nAlso on the list, below the cut: `crime_00033` — `Invoice` is declared in both `src/api/state.ts:9` and `src/billing.ts:19` and disagrees on **6 fields**, including `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `discountCents`, and `currency`. Severity 0.96, the highest score in the whole scan. It's anchored on `state.ts` so it doesn't show in `context src/billing.ts`, but `src/billing.ts` is its related file and money fields present on one side only is a genuinely bad shape for a billing module. It didn't make the top 3 only because the fix belongs in `state.ts` — it should import from here, not redeclare.",
